<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ammianus Marcellinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 330–c. 400</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ammianus Marcellinus was a Greek-born Roman soldier and historian. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> history, of which the books covering 353 to 378 survive, is the main source for the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>, Julian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>. It describes peoples on and beyond the empire's frontiers, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, Chionites and the Austuriani raiders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and ends with the Roman defeat at Adrianople.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a ... · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
